  "text_md": "# Circulation Research September 20, 2002\n\n**Effect of Treatment With SB 242784 on Soft Tissue Calcification in Rats Treated With Vitamin D**\n\nVitamin D Vitamin D110 mg Vitamin D140 mg Age-Matched Tissue, mmol Only SB 242784 SB 242784 Control Abdominal aorta 3.161.3 1.460.5* 0.360.03† 0.360.02 Kidney 39.6632.5 20.7611.9 2.660.2‡ 1.160.2 Lungs 95.5624.3 26.7633.6 3.860.3§ 3.460.5 Trachea 2.060.2 1.960.3 0.960.7i 0.660.2 Seven-week-old male rats received subcutaneous injections of 500¦000 IU of vitamin D/kg body wt at t50, 24, and 48 hours. Subsets of these animals were also injected with either 10 or 40 mg SB 242784/kg body wt per day beginning at 2 days before the first injection of vitamin D. Animals were killed 96 hours after the first vitamin D injection, and the abdominal aorta, one kidney, the lungs, and an anatomically uniform 1 cm of trachea were ashed (see Materials and Methods for further details). The ashed tissues were extracted with acid to dissolve minerals, and each acid extract was analyzed for calcium as described in Materials and Methods. Results are given as mmol calcium in the indicated tissue. Vitamin D treatment does not result in any significant increase in the size or wet weight of the tissues analyzed. Therefore, the increase in calcium content seen in the tissues from the vitamin D treatment group cannot be due to changes in the size or mass of the tissues (authors’ unpublished observations, 2002). Data are mean6SD, n54, for all groups. *P50.06 compared with vitamin D only. †P50.005 compared with vitamin D only. ‡P50.063 compared with vitamin D only. Downloaded from §P50.0003 compared with vitamin D only. iP50.023 compared with vitamin D only.\n\n|control animals (Table). Calcium levels measured on serum|||supports the hypothesis that|vitamin D treatment induces|\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n|obtained 96 hours after the first vitamin D injection were as follows: 13.461.2 mg/dL for rats treated with vitamin D only (n54), 14.060.9 mg/dL for rats treated with vitamin D plus the 10-mg dose of SB 242784 (n|||arterial calcification by accelerating shows that SB 242784 treatment dose-dependently inhibits bone resorption activity in the vitamin D–treated rat. The effect of SB 242784 on arterial calcification was also|bone resorption and|\n|D alone), 10.860.5 mg/dL for rats treated with vitamin D plus the 40-mg dose of SB 242784 (n vitamin D alone), and 10.960.3 mg/dL for untreated control rats (n54) (P,0.001 versus vitamin D alone).|||examined in rats treated inactivates the calcification-inhibitory activity of matrix Gla protein and thereby induces rapid calcification of arteries and cartilage. In the first experiment, weanling rats were treated|with warfarin, a treatment that|\n|To confirm|that SB 242784 did in|fact inhibit bone|for 1 week with warfarin alone or with warfarin plus 10 mg||\n|resorption at the doses used in the above experiments, serum samples obtained at death were analyzed to determine the level of cross-linked N-teleopeptides, a specific marker for bone resorption activity that is released during the osteoclast-|||SB 242784/kg per day. 10-mg dose of SB 242784 did not significantly inhibit arterial calcification as assessed by alizarin red staining. Subsequent analysis of bone resorption activity using the cross-linked|This experiment showed that the|\n|mediated breakdown of bone matrix collagen. As seen in|||N-teleopeptide assay showed that|the 10-mg dose of SB|\n|Figure 4, the elevated by 140% in rats treated with vitamin D alone and|level of cross-linked N-teleopeptides was||242784 also did not activity in the warfarin-treated rats. When this experiment|significantly reduce bone resorption|\n|was reduced to below vitamin D plus the higher dose of SB 242784. This result|control levels in rats treated|with|was repeated at the higher dose (40 mg/kg per day) of SB 242784, we observed that the drug strongly inhibited arterial||\n\n[http://circres.ahajournals.org/](http://circres.ahajournals.org/)\n\n54) (*P*.0.5 versus vitamin\n54) (*P*,0.01 versus\nby guest on April 19, 2017\n\n**Figure 4.** Effect of SB 242784 on bone resorption activity as measured by serum cross-linked N-teleopeptides. Serum was obtained\n\nfrom each animal 96 hours after the first vitamin D injection in the experiment described in the Figure 1 legend, and serum was also obtained from 4 age-matched control rats. Each serum sample was analyzed to determine the level of cross-linked N-teleopeptides (OSTEOMARK NTx, Ostex), a specific marker for bone resorption activity (see Materials and Methods). Results are presented as mean6SD. a, *P*,0.01 compared with control; b, *P*,0.05 compared with vitamin D only; and c, *P*,0.001 compared with vitamin D only.\n",
